hexagon: declare one row of G dirty in the fused gated delta net state step

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus the patches before this one in patches/series.

Needs patches/hexagon-spec/0001 before it. That patch already relaxes the output guard of
op_gdn_state_step, which is the kernel half of this change. Refer to "What the kernel gives"
below. Do not put this patch in patches/series before it.

Files:
    ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp  the output descriptor of HTP_OP_GDN_STATE_STEP

The problem

The host gives the DSP one htp_tensor for each output of an op, and its size field is
ggml_nbytes of the ggml tensor. htp_tensor_dirty_all records that range, and a flush of a range
sum above HEX_L2_FLUSH_ALL_THRESHOLD (4 MiB, htp/hex-utils.h:43) selects
qurt_mem_cache_clean(..., QURT_MEM_CACHE_FLUSH_INVALIDATE_ALL) over the whole data cache, plus a
new l2fetch of the footprint.

The first output of the fused state step is G, the GATED_DELTA_NET node. Its shape is
[S_v * H, 1 + K * S_v], and the op writes the first row only: htp/gated-delta-net-ops.c says
"the op writes only row 0, the attention output", and the new state goes to the second output,
the slot of the cache.

    model   K   ne of G        ggml_nbytes   above 4 MiB   for each token, all linear layers
    2B      1   2048 x 129     1,056,768     no            18 x 1.06 MB = 19.0 MB
    2B      4   2048 x 513     4,202,496     yes, 8 KB     18 x 4.20 MB = 75.6 MB
    4B      2   4096 x 257     4,210,688     yes, 16 KB    101.1 MB
    4B      4   4096 x 513     8,404,992     yes, 4.21 MB  24 x 8.40 MB = 201.7 MB

The op writes S_v * H * 4 bytes: 8 KB on the 2B and 16 KB on the 4B. Thus a decode with the
speculation switch on pays tens of megabytes of cache maintenance for each token that a decode
without the switch never pays, and it pays it because of a shape and not because of a byte.

The change

The output descriptor is a copy of G with one row. ggml_nbytes of the copy is 8,192 bytes on the
2B and 16,384 on the 4B, thus the declared range is the range that the op writes and the
threshold is far away. The copy goes through htp_opnode::add_dummy, the same mechanism that the
conv step already uses for the span of its slots, thus the node owns the copy and it stays alive
in the graph cache.

The change is safe because no node outside the chain reads the state tail of G.
ggml_hexagon_gdn_state_chain_check permits a reader of G only inside the first head_bytes bytes,
and it refuses the fusion otherwise. Thus no node needs the tail of G in the cache.

What the kernel gives

op_gdn_state_step did a check of the whole shape of its destination:

    if (dst->ne[0] != S_v * H || dst->ne[1] != 1 + (int64_t) K * S_v) {
        GDN_STATE_FAIL(GDN_STATE_ERR_DST, HTP_STATUS_INVAL_PARAMS);
    }

patches/hexagon-spec/0001 replaces it with a check that takes one row:

    if (dst->ne[0] != S_v * H || dst->ne[1] < 1 || dst->nb[0] != sizeof(float)) {

Thus the two patches are the two halves of one change, and this one must not go in without it.
Without it the op returns HTP_STATUS_INVAL_PARAMS on each token.

An alternative that needs no kernel change is a copy that keeps ne and sets nb[1] to 0:
ggml_nbytes then gives the same 8,192 bytes, and the old shape check passes. It is not in this
patch because the descriptor would then state a layout that is not the layout of the tensor.

What this does not change

This is a decode cost, not a prefill cost. The fused state step takes one token only
(htp-gdn-match.h refuses a batch), thus the prefill never reaches this code. On the prefill the
plain GATED_DELTA_NET node declares its real bytes, and those are above 4 MiB at every K,
including K = 1 at 512 tokens. That full flush of the prefill is a separate subject.

State of the verification

The Android build of the pinned container compiles the change. The arithmetic above comes from
the shapes of the two model files and from ggml_nbytes. The device has not run it, and it must
not run it before the kernel change is in.
diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp
index 6884a0d..a3dfadb 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/ggml-hexagon.cpp
@@ -6454,10 +6454,36 @@ static void ggml_hexagon_match_gdn_fusions(const ggml_hexagon_session * sess, co
                 continue;
             }
 
+            // [TAG_GDN_STATE_DIRTY]
+            // The op writes the attention row of G, which is the first S_v * H floats, and the
+            // new state into the slot of the cache. It never writes the state tail of G. But G
+            // is [S_v * H, 1 + K * S_v], thus the backend declares ggml_nbytes(G) dirty: 4.20 MB
+            // of the 2B at K = 4, and 8.40 MB of the 4B. Each of those crosses
+            // HEX_L2_FLUSH_ALL_THRESHOLD, and the DSP then cleans the whole data cache in place
+            // of the 8 KB that the op wrote. Over the linear layers of one token that is tens of
+            // megabytes of cache maintenance that a context without rollback slots never pays.
+            //
+            // The output that goes to the DSP is therefore a copy of G with one row. It is safe
+            // because ggml_hexagon_gdn_state_chain_check permits a reader of G only inside the
+            // first head_bytes bytes: no node outside the chain reads the state tail, thus no
+            // node needs it in the cache.
+            ggml_tensor head = *m.G;
+            head.ne[1]     = 1;
+            head.ne[2]     = 1;
+            head.ne[3]     = 1;
+            head.nb[2]     = head.nb[1];
+            head.nb[3]     = head.nb[1];
+            head.op        = GGML_OP_NONE;
+            head.view_src  = nullptr;
+            head.view_offs = 0;
+            for (int s = 0; s < GGML_MAX_SRC; s++) {
+                head.src[s] = nullptr;
+            }
+
             htp_opnode node(HTP_OP_GDN_STATE_STEP, m.G);
             node.name    = "GDN_STATE_STEP";
             node.inputs  = { m.q, m.k, m.v, m.g, m.beta, m.states, m.idx, m.slot };
-            node.outputs = { m.G, m.slot };
+            node.outputs = { node.add_dummy(head), m.slot };
             node.fused   = { m.R, m.P };
 
             fu.at.emplace(m.emit, std::move(node));
